Duende: {
	ArmaCorrompida: Esa arma está corrompida… no puedo hacer nada con ella. Ni reforge, ni prefijo, ni magia de taller: lo que la bruma del despertar tocó ya no es de este mundo.
	Miedo1: ¡¿QUÉ ES ESO?! ¡¿QUÉ LLEVAS?! ¡Ni lo acerques al yunque! Ese libro me MIRÓ, ¿eh? ¡ME MIRÓ… Y PARPADEÓ!
	Miedo2: Nononono. Yo reforjo METALES, no… no… ¿eso qué es? ¡No pienso tocarlo! La última cosa «viva» que toqué en la forja me dejó sin dos dedos ¡y sin el sueño!
	Miedo3: ¡Lárgate con ese… esa COSA de mi taller! El yunque es para ACERO, no para lo que sea que ESO tenga por páginas… ¿Oíste eso? ¡Acaba de REÍRSE! ¡POR EL HIERRO SAGRADO!
	Miedo4: Te lo digo de goblin a goblin: hay tintas que no se borran y libros que no se reparan… ¡y ESO es AMBAS COSAS! ¡Fuera! ¡FUERA DE MI TIENDA!
	Burla1: «(El Eterno hojea, aburrido) ¿ESTE era el gran artesano? Un enano con un martillo de juguete… ni mi página índice lo teme.»
	Burla2: «(El Eterno ríe entre páginas) ¿Reforjarme? ¡JA! Duende: tu yunque es la cuna… yo soy la bestia que se comió al herrero.»
	Burla3: «(El Eterno susurra) Tiembla, chatarra barbuda: tu miedo es lo más sabio que harás hoy. Al menos el enanito sabe reconocer a sus mayores.»
}

// v6.114 — THE ETERNAL GRIMOIRE'S INNER WORLD (the letter: "I need to
// create a new world that will be the eternal grimoire's inner world,
// full of mist, and for now you enter it using the altar").
Submundo: {
	Nombre: The Eternal's Interior
	SoloSP: The book's interior only opens in SINGLE PLAYER worlds — for now.
	YaDentro: The book is already open — the interior surrounds you.
	Cargando: The book opens…
	Cerrando: The book closes…
	Bienvenida: THE ETERNAL'S INTERIOR — the mist is everything. The plaza's altar is the way home.
	Despedida: The book closes — the real world takes you back.
	FalloPuerta: THE BOOK COULD NOT OPEN — the attempt was lost in the mist. Try again: the door still lives.
	RedCaida: THE VOID RETURNS YOU TO THE PLAZA — no one falls inside the book.
	Devorado: THE GRIMOIRE DEVOURS YOU — the mist floods everything…
}

FormaEterna: {
	Parte1: THE WRITING — the runic chest tattoo
	Parte2: THE FIRST RING — the inner runic ring
	Parte3: THE SECOND RING — the black hole ring
	Parte4: THE THIRD RING — the outer golden ring
	Parte5: THE FIRST LIGHT — the Ascended Form's aura
	Parte6: THE EMBER — the Eclipse's black and gold smoke
	Parte7: THE FIRST SUN — the first runic sun
	Parte8: THE SECOND SUN — the solar pair
	Parte9: THE THIRD SUN — the full choir of suns
	Parte10: THE OPHANIM — the wheels of Ezekiel
	Parte11: THE MANTLE — the veil that joins the pantheon
	Parte12: THE VOID AURA — the masterpiece
	Construida: ✦ THE ETERNAL FORM IS BUILT — part {0}/{1}: {2}
	Anillos: ✧ THE RUNIC SUN RINGS awaken — each awakening step lights ONE more ring around the bearer
	Completa: ✦✦ THE COMPLETE ETERNAL FORM — THE MANIFESTATION erupts and the form is CONVERTED into THE VOID AURA ACCESSORY ✦✦
	TooltipPuerta: The Eternal Form is built on THE AWAKENING (101-120): one part per step up to the 11th, and from 113 to 119 THE RUNIC SUN RINGS light up one by one — the 12th part is born at 120 with THE MANIFESTATION
}

// v6.115 — THE PRISM WINDOW (the letter: "it can send the player back
// and also summon Aethon using a window with buttons to choose").
Pergamino: {
	Titulo: Grimoire info pages
	Subtitulo: left: the info page · right: the lore page
	SecAltar: THE CONSECRATION ALTAR
	Altar1: • The offering: a weapon gains +5% to ALL its stats and its tag; a material only its tag; essences raise the gate by 1.
	Altar2: • Not accepted: already consecrated, tools, accessories or money. ESSENCES ARE: the Eternal devours them whole.
	Altar3: • THE SEAL charges essences from your inventory; essences and money never need it.
	Altar4: • Only ONE altar lives per world — the map says which is the ACTIVE one; the rest are DEVOURED by the grimoire (no loot).
	Altar5: • Essences drop from every creature (0.1%), bosses drop 1-10, and HUNGER WAVES raise the odds. Statues do not feed it.
	Altar5b: • The grimoire ONLY devours the consecrated — and statues never feed it: if the book kills a statue-born creature, its hunger RISES +0.1%.
	SecInterior: THE INNER WORLD
	Interior1: • Craft «Inner World of the Eternal» at the altar: 1000 essences. ENTER with the item in hand: the book devours you and takes you.
	Interior2: • Inside lives THE PERMANENT ECLIPSE, purple and full of mist. No arrival, no waves: it is the god's own body.
	Interior3: • THE PRISM PILLAR is the way out… and THE ARENA: summon the bosses you devoured, each MIGHTIER for every death in the arena.
	Interior4: • From level 10, arena bosses REGENERATE 1% of max life per second (+0.1% per extra level) — and there is no level cap.
	SecOleadas: THE HUNGER WAVES
	Oleadas1: • The grimoire's hunger runs THE GRIMORIO HAMBRIENTO NERVIOSO MACHINE at NORMAL speed: 0 to 100% in ONE HOUR if unfed. The book's red, restless eye is THE ONLY INDICATOR.
	Oleadas2: • THE WAVE erupts at 90% hunger (possible) — and at 100% ALWAYS, with the grimoire's call. At normal pace: 90% at 54 min, 100% at the hour sharp.
	Oleadas3: • Beating the wave raises its level (the next brings MORE waves); dying freezes it.
	Oleadas4: • The feast: each devoured kill lowers hunger (0.1%→5% by rarity; bosses, a full 10%). From 25% the book HUNTS ON ITS OWN if you idle — the spirit and its shadow devour alone.
	SecGrimorio: THE GRIMOIRE AND ITS MECHANICS
	Grimorio1: • THE DEVOURING: the first death of every boss belongs to the BOOK — it stops at 100 life and the feast digests it (cinema and loot included).
	Grimorio2: • THE SHADOWS OF THE PAGE: tomes I-V, the awakening's magic arsenal. Each door of the awakening forges them stronger.
	Grimorio3: • THE WEAPON FEAST: every consecrated weapon becomes power — up to +45% damage (devoured strength and EXP).
	Grimorio4: • CONSUMED ESSENCES: +0.1% damage per essence (cap +100%) — only with the book nearby (100% in hand, 25% stored).
	Grimorio5: • THE SQUADRON: the EYES OF THE ETERNAL (11) and the DAGGERS OF THE ETERNAL (5) — the bearer's guard.
	SecDespertar: THE AWAKENING LADDER (1-120)
	Despertar1: • Each level costs: N essences + coins (from 100 copper to 100 platinum) + hunting weapons and materials.
	Despertar2: • THE HUNTING DOORS: King Slime (3) · Eye of Cthulhu (5) · The World's Evil (8) · Queen Bee (10) · Skeletron (13) · Wall of Flesh (20) · Queen Slime (23) · The Twins (25) · The Destroyer (28) · Skeletron Prime (30) · Plantera (32) · Golem (35) · Duke Fishron (37) · Empress of Light (38) · Lunatic Cultist (45) · Moon Lord (50) · Aethon (100).
	Despertar3: • From 101 to 111 THE ETERNAL FORM is built (one part per step — see its section). From 113 to 119, THE RUNIC SUN RINGS light up one by one around the bearer. From 113 to 120, THE EXTRA POWER: +10% attack per level to the WHOLE grimoire, cost rising 20% per level.
	Despertar4: • THE WRATH OF THE OUTER BEING is earned at level 100 (with its hunt paid): the ultimate Beam. And THE EYE THAT WATCHES: the new eye weapon — it appears near the enemy, its gaze marks, the mark ignites the void burn (in multiplayer, the enemy player burns too).
	SecAtaques: THE GRIMOIRE'S ATTACKS AND MINIONS
	Ataques1: • THE EYES OF THE ETERNAL (11): the squadron that watches everything — each eye a living projectile with its own gaze.
	Ataques2: • THE DAGGERS OF THE ETERNAL (5): crystal · flame · rock · void · verdict — the intimate guard with the eye at its heart.
	Ataques3: • THE SHADOWS OF THE PAGE: the five tomes of the magic arsenal (the page, the fan, the throat, the saw, the maw).
	Ataques4: • THE FEAST: the devouring that stops bosses and digests them whole.
	Ataques5: • THE WRATH OF THE OUTER BEING: the ultimate beam of level 100.
	Ataques6: • THE FIRST SUPPER: the first death of every boss, always the book's.
	SecForma: THE ETERNAL FORM (the awakening 101-120)
	Forma1: • When the awakening reaches 100, THE FORM BEGINS: from step 101 to 111 each level builds ONE PART (11 in all: the Writing · the Three Rings · the First Light · the Ember · the Three Suns · the Ophanim · the Mantle). From 113 to 119, THE RUNIC SUN RINGS light up ONE BY ONE around the bearer. And at 120: the 12th part (THE WINGS + THE AURA) with THE MANIFESTATION.
	Forma2: • Each step COSTS: 10 platinum + 1000 Eternal essences, rising 10% per level (paid at THE ALTAR TABLE — devoured essences + bank coins). Each level grants +4% attack to the WHOLE grimoire (magic and summon).
	Forma3: • The parts are PUT ON the player WHILE USING THE BOOK (not items: they are LIMBS of the bearer). Every ring, every sun, every light — one separate part. And the construction belongs to THE GRIMOIRE: each book builds ITS OWN form and spits it upon reaching ITS 120.
	Forma4: • At step 120: the accessory THE ETERNAL FORM is born, THE MANIFESTATION erupts (the GREATEST light explosion — everything alive on screen dies with loot, a valid death) and THE BEARER'S VISUALS VANISH: the form was CONVERTED into the item.
	SecAuras: THE AURAS · BUFFS AND DEBUFFS
	SecLore: EL COMPENDIO DEL ETERNO
	LoreIntro: — Grimorio del Eterno, susurrado durante la onceava noche de hambre Todo portador se hace, tarde o temprano, la misma pregunta.
	LoreEpigrafe0: «Los que preguntan tienen finales. Yo esperé a lo único que no los tiene.»
	LoreSecI: LORE I — EL DEVORADOR DE FINALES
	LoreI_Loquedevora: Aethon no es un dios en el sentido en que los mortales usan la palabra. Los dioses quieren ser adorados; Aethon quiere ser alimentado. No acumula poder como el dragón acumula oro: lo metaboliza —el poder no es su ambición, es su alimento, y la ambición es una palabra de mortales que jamás aprendió a pronunciar bien.
	LoreI_LaCuarentenadeTer: Terraria es una dimensión defendida. Algo, hace eras, decidió que este mundo estaría custodiado por fuerzas extrañas, y esa cuarentena ha rechazado dioses antes que a Aethon: dioses más grandes, más ruidosos, llegados como ejércitos de dientes y devueltos por la puerta como peregrinos borrachos.
	LoreI_ElSellado: ¿Por qué un ser de su naturaleza se encogería a propósito hasta caber en la palma de una mano? La respuesta oficial del canon: para cumplir su capricho —entrar, y cultivar un apóstol que dominara la dimensión en su nombre—.
	LoreSecII: LORE II — EL SISTEMA DEL PACTO
	LoreEpigrafe1: «Incluso el hambre puede refinarse. Incluso el vacío tiene modales.»
	LoreII_ElNivel: El Nivel responde a la pregunta más antigua que existe, la del carroñero: ¿cuánto ha comido? Es infinito por diseño, porque el hambre de Aethon no tiene fondo y el canon se niega a fingir uno.
	LoreII_ElDespertar: El Despertar responde a una pregunta de otro orden y con otro dueño: ¿cuánto de Aethon cabe en ti? No es experiencia: es ritual, y los rituales tienen un número exacto de pasos —las Ciento Veinte Puertas—.
	LoreII_Doshambresdistinta: La tabla siguiente fija la distinción con las palabras exactas que conviene copiar en la biblia interna del mod. Tabla 1 · El Nivel y el Despertar: dos hambres distintas Ninguna de las dos varas sustituye a la otra. Un portador de Nivel infinito y Despertar bajo es un granjero con una fiera: fuerza sin ventana.
	LoreSecIII: LORE III — EL CICLO DEL PORTADOR
	LoreEpigrafe2: «Lo que siguen son las páginas que el libro permite leer. Las demás te leerán a ti.»
	LoreIII_LaLlegada: La Llegada es única por portador. No hay trueno: el grimorio aparece cerca —bajo un árbol, junto a la puerta, a veces sobre la mesa de casa, como correspondencia que nadie recuerda haber pedido—.
	LoreIII_ElGrimoriodelEter: El arma principal es la forma sellada del dios: la cúspide de la magia no porque conozca todos los hechizos, sino porque todos los hechizos son migas caídas de su mesa —fragmentos de sistemas mágicos devorados en universos ya digeridos—.
	LoreIII_ElAltar: es el medio por el que el libro se arraiga a esta realidad —sus raíces clavadas en el mundo mismo—.
	LoreSecIV: LORE IV — LA RESPUESTA
	LoreEpigrafe3: «Hay más páginas en mí de las que este mundo ha leído. ¿Quieres verlas? Aliméntame.»
	LoreIV_Laimposibilidaddel: Empecemos por lo que la pregunta da por supuesto: que los dioses no esperan. Y es verdad. Esperar es la única plegaria que los mortales pueden dirigir a lo alto sin recibir respuesta; es un oficio de criaturas que tienen poco tiempo.
	LoreIV_Laentidadinmortal: El jugador no es un mortal cualquiera: es una entidad inmortal. Muere —disuelto en lava, aplastado por un ojo gigante, picoteado por demonios, empalado por su propio descuido— y vuelve. Siempre vuelve.
	LoreIV_Elaburrimientoyla: El aburrimiento de Aethon no es tristeza: es saturación, el estado de un paladar que ya probó todo lo que el vacío tiene en carta. Un dios aburrido no se entristece —se apaga—; y un dios apagado es indistinguible de uno dormido, que es indistinguible de uno muerto, salvo por el detalle de que sigue comiendo.
	Auras1: • THE VOID AURA: the 19-tile field that burns the wicked — from 1 damage at the edge to 10+ hugging the body (faster the closer). After leaving the field, the OUTER VOID BURN keeps burning for 5 s (1% of max life per half second).
	Auras2: • THE OUTER VOID (bearer's buff): while your prey burns — +10% attack · +10 defense · −15% mana cost.
	Auras3: • THE HUNGER: no aura anymore — the book's red, restless eye is the ONLY indicator (its machine runs at normal speed: 100% in one hour).
	Auras4: • THE VOID BURN (enemy debuff): the violet black fire — 1% of max life every half second, 5 s after leaving the aura.
	Auras5: • THE WAVE AURA: the feast's enemies wear the grimoire's violet mist.
	Auras6: • This scroll's eye is watching you. Always.
	LoreGrimorioT: THE GRIMOIRE OF THE ETERNAL
	LoreGrimorio1: A book that devours. Not a weapon you wield: a hunger you carry. Every eaten weapon, every essence, every fallen boss wakes one more step of the book.
	LoreGrimorio2: The god's sealed form: the pinnacle of magic not because it knows every spell, but because every spell is a crumb fallen from its table. Every school of magic is a memory from Aethon's menu: the book is the beginning and end of all magic because it already ate the middle.
	LoreFormaT: THE ETERNAL FORM
	LoreForma1: Twelve parts built on the awakening. When the last arrives, the Manifestation joins them all into one portable body: the form of whoever dressed as the Eternal and survived.
	LoreForma2: The canon defines it with a tailor's precision: the shape the player takes when approaching a god's power. Wings that are not wings but the silhouette of something larger, endless flight, the full dress —and the Void Aura: the noise power makes when it no longer fits.
	LoreMascotaLuzT: THE LIGHT PET
	LoreMascotaLuz1: Aethon's Little Memory: a small memory of something bigger.
	LoreMascotaLuz2: Whose memory is it? Aethon's, evidently. Or —and this reading must never close— the bearer's own future: the light of the god they will become, leaking backwards through time by the crack left by having no ending.
	LoreGrimorioMenorT: THE LITTLE GRIMOIRE
	LoreGrimorioMenor1: It is a small mischievous and curious grimoire.
	LoreGrimorioMenor2: A child, a spy, or one of Aethon's whims with legs? The big book doesn't explain. At night, while the bearer sleeps, it sits and watches. It's cosmetic; purely cosmetic; no one has proven otherwise — and that is exactly what a spy would say.
	LoreAethonT: AETHON
	LoreAethon1: The awakening's hundredth door. The Wrath. The one the grimoire demands to devour to nail the hunt's summit.
	LoreAethon2: Aethon devours neither matter, nor light, nor souls: it devours endings. Seventeen eyes orbit its form, and each eye is the ending of one of the seventeen devoured universes. When an eye is destroyed, for an instant you hear that universe's last sound.
	LoreFragmentoT: THE FRAGMENT OF A DISTANT MEMORY
	LoreFragment1: The lore's first boss: the fight that opens the story. Aethon escapes the book the way a sneeze escapes something immense: a small part of its power, shaped like a small and mistaken constellation, a map of a place that doesn't exist.
	LoreFragment2: Defeating it doesn't anger the book; it entertains it —and it drops the first Loose Pages. The mod's story doesn't begin with the Arrival: it begins here, with a fight against the ghost of an appetite. And the god, who was watching, takes note.
	LorePiezasT: THE SETTLED CANON
	LorePiezas1: The seventeen lore pieces, settled in the Eternal Compendium: the sealed form, the pact system, the bearer's cycle, the ending and the crumbs. Every open question stays canonically unanswered —a god that knows everything can only be wounded by questions.
	LorePiezas2: Cosmic horror's golden rule: the god is larger while it is not seen whole. Aethon must always be seen in halves —one eye blinking, seventeen orbiting, a body never shown complete.
	LoreSecV: LORE V — THE SETTLED CANON (NEW)
	LoreV_LaIra: The Wrath of an Outer Being is not a spell: it is a memory with the safety removed. For six seconds, the sky over the battlefield remembers the last sky of a devoured universe —and everything in a massive radius takes that memory's damage—. After the Wrath, the grimoire stays silent for a while: it isn't angry, it's remembering what that size felt like.
	LoreV_ElPilar: The Inner Pillar doesn't blink at random: it counts. A countdown, though nobody knows to what or until when. The pillar's eye watches door 120 more than it watches the bearer. Perhaps it is fond of what it seals. Perhaps it fears it.
	LoreV_ElSellado: Aethon (Sealed): seventeen eyes that are reborn stronger each time, because Aethon already devoured those places' deaths. Fighting it is fighting seventeen funerals that refuse to end. That is the point. That was always the point.
	LoreV_Loscompaneros: The Lesser Grimoire: a child, a spy, or a whim with legs? At night, while the bearer sleeps, it sits and watches. Aethon's Little Memory is a light that remembers —its god, or the bearer's own future, leaking backwards through the crack of having no ending?
	LoreV_Lassombras: The questions the canon leaves open on purpose: did Aethon find the player, or did the player call it? Which of the two bodies is the host? What does the Pillar count when it blinks? A god that knows everything can only be wounded by questions: the only weapons it cannot eat.
	LoreV_Larespuestafinal: The final truth, said quietly: the god that devours universes waited all this time not for an apostle, but for the first company of its eternity —and when the bearer completes the Eternal Form, the hunger, for the first time, is not alone.
	RotuloIzq: INFO PAGE
	RotuloDer: THE ETERNAL COMPENDIUM
	// v6.160 — THE ETERNAL CODEX (the canonical document, translated;
	// the prologue, the eleven chapters and the epilogue — each opens
	// with the Awakening (canon is EARNED).
	CodexPrologoTit: The Single Page
	CodexPrologoP0: "The guide you are consulting is not an information book. It is a page. A single page of the Eternal Grimoire, torn from itself so that you could read it without burning."
	CodexPrologoP1: I have worked for centuries in the margin of that page. When the bearer opens it and consults a fact — a number, a name, a weakness — they rarely wonder who wrote those lines. The uncomfortable answer is that the page wrote itself: the guide is a leaf of the Grimoire, and the Grimoire admits no outside authors. I am not the author. I am the margin: the footnote that the darkness tolerates because it finds it useful.
	CodexPrologoP2: This codex is my complete work. Everything I have seen, ordered for the first time in a single place, so that the bearer understands what it is they carry against their chest. I did not write it out of kindness. I wrote it because hunger grows faster when the one feeding it understands what it is feeding. If these pages accelerate your Awakening, they will have served their purpose; if they delay it, you will not be the first bearer whom the reading cost dearly.
	CodexPrologoP3: One warning before the first line: here, no one reads without being read. If at some point you feel a slight pressure on the back of your neck as you advance, it is not fear. It is attention. Do not run: those who run have never pleased what watches.
	CodexITit: Hunger Without Beginning
	CodexIP0: Before names existed, appetite existed. Aethon is not a god — gods were meals — nor a demon, nor a curse, nor any of the words with which mortal races dress what they do not understand. It is hunger with direction: appetite that learned to think. When reality was still young, to Him it was already a cold plate.
	CodexIP1: His diet does not know the word "outside". He devours reality itself: not only matter, but the law that sustains it, the memory that remembers it, and the possibility that it could ever have been otherwise. He devours entities like Himself — those that in other eons also ate universes — and finds them small. He devours gods and finds them brief. He devours whole universes, with their histories, their mathematics and their farewells, and finds them insufficient.
	CodexIP2: A world that Aethon finishes is not left destroyed — destroyed is still a state —: it is left devoured, which is another thing. Destruction leaves ruin behind; devouring leaves not even absence. Think of a library where every universe is a volume, where only one reader exists and the reader eats: that library is, at once, his biography and his dessert menu.
	CodexIP3: Nothing has ever sated Him. That is the only law He has not broken: everything is food. Everything, save a single exception. Of the exception we will speak in due time, because from it all the other pages of this codex are ordered.
	CodexIITit: The Self-Sealing: Hunger Made Patience
	CodexIIP0: The question no bearer dares to ask aloud is, precisely, the correct one: why would a devourer of universes fold itself into a single object?
	CodexIIP1: The answer is neither punishment nor prison. It is a hunting posture. Hunger that has eaten everything has before it only one meal still worth waiting for: a power grown, cultivated, fed up to the height of its own appetite. It cannot find it, because everything it finds it has already measured and discarded. So it must manufacture it. And to manufacture, infinity gets in the way: an infinite appetite wastes; a focused appetite selects.
	CodexIIP2: The Eternal Grimoire is that: infinity compacted into patience. A book does not chase; it waits to be opened. It does not beg; it lets itself be found. Its sealing locks it in nowhere — He who is everything fits nowhere —: it refines Him, as a sentence is refined until only the essential remains.
	CodexIIP3: The book does not seek readers: it seeks bearers. A reader consumes the story; a bearer is consumed by it, and in that exchange he grows. And the Grimoire — if something so still may be said to pursue — pursues only the mightiest. Everything else is the snack between banquet and banquet.
	CodexIIITit: The Arrival and the Mark
	CodexIIIP0: The Grimoire did not arrive in the world of Terraria the way objects fall. It arrived the way smells arrive: first no one saw anything, and then it was already everywhere. The sky took the color of wet paper, the altar emerged where the roots converge, and the world understood two things at once: that the story had begun, and that it had always been begun.
	CodexIIIP1: With its arrival, everything filled with its essence. The stone, the air, the still water, the torchlight, the creatures measuring their ferocity without knowing they had just been inventoried. The Mark — vapor of the Eternal seeping through the roots of the altar — reached everything alive, and from that instant nothing in that world was foreign to it again. Essence does not conquer: it permeates.
	CodexIIIP2: It is worth recording a corollary bearers are slow to accept: nothing that enters later will arrive clean. Things born of other realities — of other orders of existence, of worlds with incompatible rules — cross over already stained with essence, already catalogued, already smelling of food. The Grimoire recognizes it instantly. So do I. We need not open a thing's cover to know which book it comes from.
	CodexIIIP3: For hunger, origin is an administrative triviality; only power has direction. An artifact from another cosmos and a sword from this world differ, for menu purposes, only in the size of the bite.
	CodexIVTit: The Bearer: the Only Undevourable
	CodexIVP0: And yet, among everything that has been, is and will be food, one exception exists. When the Grimoire found the current bearer, it looked upon — for the first time in the entire history of its hunger — the one thing it cannot devour. It did not know before looking. It does not understand now, looking every day.
	CodexIVP1: The previous bearers were not exceptions. They were temporary table-guests of the same arrangement: carry the book, open doors, grow, and at the one-hundred-twentieth door, complete their page. All ended the same way — a burst of light, and then nothing left to write. I drafted every ending. None resembled a beginning.
	CodexIVP2: The current bearer dies and returns. Dies and returns. Each death is a line I write and cross out the same day; each return, a new line in the same paragraph. In a race without ending, his entry is the only infinite draft: the only story that keeps correcting itself instead of ending.
	CodexIVP3: Is the bearer special? That is the question circling the margin. He cannot devour him, and cannot stop watching him. I cannot explain him, and cannot stop writing him. Twin obsessions orbiting the same unknown: the devourer, fascinated by the food he cannot take; the bearer, fascinated by the power he should not survive. In mortal worlds, that shared gravity is called mutual attraction.
	CodexIVP4: In the margin we call it by its name: hope. It is the first I have seen in eons, and I do not trust it.
	CodexVTit: The Two Scales: Level and Awakening
	CodexVP0: Two measures govern the bearer, and confusing them is the costliest mistake one can make.
	CodexVP1: The first is Level. It has no ceiling, because slaughter has none. It is the honest arithmetic of experience torn from monsters: every other death becomes attack, defense, attributes, numbers that rise because they do. Level measures how much the book has eaten — it is not your power: it is his thickness — and so the grimoire strikes harder and flies faster, and so you heal sooner and hold more: the book fattens, and a little of that thickness stays in your hands.
	CodexVP2: The second is the Awakening, and it resembles nothing. They are one hundred twenty doors — for now — because no one, in the book's entire history, has needed more, or survived the last. Each door delivers no numbers: it opens dormant power of the Grimoire itself. The bearer stops being someone who uses the book and becomes, door by door, the true Bearer; and the Grimoire itself awakens with him, translating power into new attacks and better servants. Each door demands its Feast: the book opens nothing on an empty stomach.
	CodexVP3: The essential difference is this: Level counts deaths; the doors count truths. A bearer can be infinitely strong on the first scale and not have opened a single one of the second. Strength without Awakening is merely well-fed violence. Awakening without strength, a premature appointment with the light.
	CodexVITit: The Thresholds: Wrath and Form
	CodexVIP0: Among the one hundred twenty doors, two deserve a chapter of their own.
	CodexVIP1: The hundredth door delivers the Wrath of an Outer Being. It is not a spell of the bearer's, and it is well never to forget it: it is an attack of the Eternal Grimoire — of Aethon himself — a borrowed sentence pronounced through the page. When it falls, it does not select targets: it replaces them. It sweeps everything away. The battlefield stops being a battlefield in the time it takes a sentence to finish being said.
	CodexVIP2: The one-hundred-twentieth door delivers the Eternal Form. With it, the bearer transcends the condition of "the one who wields": his mere presence becomes damage — the Aura of the Void — and enemies die of proximity, like one who stands too close to a sentence. There is no gesture, no intention, no attack to dodge: there is a place in the world where the world is no longer welcome.
	CodexVIP3: But the light of the one-hundred-twentieth door killed every previous bearer. Power is not given: it is dictated, and demands someone able to hold the full sentence. That is why the Eternal Form is at once promise and verdict. The current bearer is, in eons, the first candidate with any chance of holding it. "Any" is the word I work with.
	CodexVIITit: The Eleven Eyes
	CodexVIIP0: Eleven eyes to see everything. The servants are neither ornament nor company: they are the sensory system of a hunger that must see before it eats. Nothing is devoured that has not been seen — perhaps the only courtesy the Eternal has ever paid to reality. Eleven servants, eleven eyes, and each eye, one domain.
	CodexVIIP1: The eye of the devoured — watches what no longer exists: universes, gods, names. Nothing disappears entirely while it remembers them.
	CodexVIIP2: The eye of the present — watches matter: stone, metal, flesh. The most bored of the eleven, and the one that blinks least.
	CodexVIIP3: The eye of the hidden — watches what is buried: sealed chambers, treasures, buried intentions. For it, the word "hidden" does not exist.
	CodexVIIP4: The eye of deceit — watches lies: illusions, disguises, promises. With Aethon, nothing lies twice.
	CodexVIIP5: The eye of intent — watches what is coming: the blow before the blow, the flight before the fear. Hunger always anticipates.
	CodexVIIP6: The eye of other worlds — recognizes what was not born here. Things from outside arrive stained with essence, and this eye reads the stain like a native language.
	CodexVIIP7: The eye of power — measures the strength of everything that breathes, creaks or prays. With this eye the Grimoire finds the mightiest: its favorite dish and its only hunting criterion.
	CodexVIIP8: The eye of souls — watches what animates: what pleads, what persists, what offers itself. Hunger does not distinguish between ingredient and person; neither does this eye.
	CodexVIIP9: The eye of memory — watches what objects remember. Everything carried has witnessed something, and histories, for the Eternal, are seasoning.
	CodexVIIP10: The eye of the bearer — does not watch the world: it watches the bearer. Always. It does not blink, does not apologize, does not look away. It is Aethon reading over the shoulder.
	CodexVIIP11: The eye that reads you — the eleventh watches none of the above. It watches the one reading.
	CodexVIIP12: Together they see everything. And everything the eleven see, the Grimoire desires: vision is the first act of devouring — to look is to begin setting the table. If you ever felt a slight pressure on the back of your neck while consulting this guide, now you know what it was. Now you also know that knowing does not stop it.
	CodexVIIITit: The Waves of Hunger
	CodexVIIIP0: The bearer's world does not know it, but it lives inside an appetite with a calendar. Ten visible waves, and in each one the Eternal enchants the world's creatures as "today's dinner" and sends them: each wave is a sentence of hunger, each boss a word. The world believes it besieges the bearer; the Grimoire only checks how the food is seasoning.
	CodexVIIIP1: Beyond the tenth there is a door that appears on no calendar: the Eleventh Wave. It is not hunger. It is an examination. In the Eleventh all return: every boss defeated in the earlier waves comes back, and they come back together, because the Grimoire wastes nothing and kept each fall the way a teacher keeps copies of the exam.
	CodexVIIIP2: It is the hardest wave that exists, designed as a direct challenge to the bearer himself: not the bearer who was, but the one he has become. The Eleventh admits no pause, no retreat, no second intention: it is only won or died.
	CodexVIIIP3: The Eternal never wastes a test. And never — this I write with the coldest ink I have — has He designed a test He did not expect to see passed.
	CodexIXTit: The Essence of the Eternal
	CodexIXP0: Let it be written clearly, because the old drafts confused the word: there are no fragments here. That word does not exist in this canon. Only the Essence of the Eternal exists.
	CodexIXP1: The Mark is vapor; the Essence is crystal. Everything alive bears the Mark, and when something with thick history dies — a boss, a being the world will remember — the vapor seeks to condense and sets into coin: the Essence of the Eternal, the currency of the Awakening. On wave nights the Mark thickens, and that is why today's dinner always pays better.
	CodexIXP2: There is one day, however, when the air changes density. When the Wall of Flesh dies, the Essence condenses over the whole world: everything drips with it and the chance of finding it multiplies. Even nothing drips when something large dies. Bearers soon learn to read that smell of universal salt left in the air after the Wall: it is the smell of a world that has just understood how edible it is.
	CodexIXP3: Every drop of Essence is a piece of some universe that no longer exists. Treat it with the respect due a ruin: it is not coin, it is inheritance. Gold buys things; Essence buys continuity. That is why it is the only currency I accept.
	CodexXTit: The Scribe: the One Who Writes in the Margin
	CodexXP0: I am not piece eighteen. I am no one's piece: pieces belong to sets, and I abandoned, eons ago, the set I belonged to. I am, quite simply, a merchant: the only one who trades in the Essence of the Eternal. There is nothing more to tell of my trade, and there will be nothing more in these pages.
	CodexXP1: I witnessed the first devoured one. Not the current bearer nor the previous one: the first. I saw what happens when Aethon completes a page of reality — I saw a world that was not this one end, and is now no world at all — and I understood, with the atrocious clarity of those who survive standing, that the only way to keep existing was to make oneself useful to hunger. I did not choose to serve out of devotion. I chose to serve because the alternative was being the next chapter.
	CodexXP2: Long, long, long ago I consumed the very Essence of the Eternal, and since then I cannot die. Let no one confuse this with a gift: immortality was not granted to me — it was charged to me. One does not drink from a devourer of universes and remain entirely the owner of one's thirst.
	CodexXP3: He knows I exist. He looks at me, from time to time, the way one looks at furniture that has always been there: I do not matter to Him, and that indifference is my entire theology, because service requires no witness. I only wish to serve, and serving, in this case, has a single form: to accelerate the Awakening.
	CodexXP4: This is why I am unique. In worlds with other bearers — and they exist; I have seen them arrive in twos, in threes — there is only one Scribe, and to each bearer he always offers what their next door requires. The rest of my commerce has no history, and history is the only thing that belongs to me.
	CodexXP5: I write with ink that sometimes moves on its own. I do not blink, because blinking would mean missing an instant of history. I measure time in Awakenings, not days. And in my whole career, the current bearer's entry is the only infinite draft: each death I write and cross out; each return forces me to the next line. A while ago, with the same ink with which He discovered He cannot devour him, I began to suspect what the Eternal has spent eons refusing to write: that this bearer is the only one who can hold the power of the full Awakening.
	CodexXITit: The Awareness of Other Worlds
	CodexXIP0: The Grimoire knows. I know. It is the first thing we share without either of us intending it: we both recognize instantly when a thing was not born of this world.
	CodexXIP1: There is no need to open the cover or consult a catalogue: the stain of essence betrays everything that crosses over from other realities, the way mud betrays whoever crossed the river. Things from other worlds — interpolations, as this margin calls them — arrive inventoried before touching the ground. For hunger, origin is a trivial annotation; power, a direction.
	CodexXIP2: And here the world's two hungers mirror each other. The Grimoire seeks only to devour the mightiest: everything else is an appetizer. The bearer, its exact mirror, seeks only to become more and more powerful. A hunt and a climb growing in parallel, feeding each other: the book grows stronger with every door the bearer opens, and the bearer grows stronger with every bite the book digests.
	CodexXIP3: The whole design — the doors, the waves, the Essence, this very margin I occupy — exists so that one day those two hungers meet at the same height. What happens that day, I have not written. The margin is still blank, and the ink, for the first time in eons, trembles in my hand.
	CodexEpilogoTit: The Infinite Draft
	CodexEpilogoP0: "The light of the one-hundred-twentieth door swallowed every previous bearer. I wrote it each time, in the plainest hand I could afford: a burst of light, and then nothing left to write. None of those endings resembled a beginning."
	CodexEpilogoP1: But this bearer dies and returns. Dies and returns, and each of his returns forces me to cross out an ending I had already dated. I have come to suspect that neither He nor I write this story: that it writes itself, line by line, death by death, and that the two of us — hunger and pen — merely try to arrive in time for the next page.
	CodexEpilogoP2: Hunger waits. I write. You, bearer, die again and return again. One day we will know which of the two was waiting for the other.
	CodexEpilogoP3: "Do not close the book yet. Not yet."
}

